  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nWednesday - Friday: Cooling Down with Shower Chances Wednesday\n\nNorthwesterly synoptic flow in the presence of an incoming \nprogressive shortwave trough will be the name of the game for the \nremainder of the week. Consequently, as this disturbance treks \nthrough the region, expecting some increase for shower chances \nduring the day on Wednesday. As we head into Wednesday morning, a \nweak surface low develops at the exit region of this aforementioned \nshortwave trough across portions of central IA. As this occurs, weak \nisentropic upglide on the eastern periphery of this low will \nallow for a brief and weak axis of 700-800mb frontogenesis \nacross portions of north-central WI on Wednesday morning. As a \nresult, the recent NBM has respectable probabilities (20-50%) \nfor measurable precipitation, however given the weak forcing, \nprobabilities for over 0.1\" during this timeframe remain low \n(under 20%). By the time Wednesday evening and overnight roll \naround, cold air advection within the northwesterly/northerly \nsynoptic flow regime will allow for cooling mid-levels, both \nsteepening low-level lapse rates and increasing low-level \ninstability during this timeframe. Consequently, some additional\nshowers cannot be ruled out late Wednesday evening through \nearly Thursday morning as shown in the 10.12z HRRR. While \nminimal precipitation amounts would be expected with these, a \nfew snowflakes could mix in early on Thursday morning as surface\ntemperatures cool for areas north of I-90 in western Wisconsin.\nHowever, no snow accumulations are expected with any light snow\nshowers that occur.\n\nAs far as temperatures are concerned, relatively mild high \ntemperatures on Wednesday will give way to much cooler temperatures \nfor Thursday and Friday as cold air advection in the wake of the \naforementioned shortwave trough pushes into the region. \nConsequently, highs in the upper 40s to middle 50s on Wednesday will\ngive way to high temperatures falling into the upper 30s and \nlower 40s for Thursday and Friday. Given that 850mb temperatures\nin deterministic guidance range in the -7C to -10C range for \nFriday, thinking that temperatures may be slightly cooler with \nthe NBM bias correction keeping temperatures warmer than \ndeterministic guidance. As a result, opted to trend temperatures\nslightly cooler than the NBM for Friday with much of the area \nstaying in the 30s for highs. \n\nThis Weekend - Early Next Week: Dry and Gradually Warming  \n\nBy the time we reach the weekend, upper-level ridging will attempt \nto push into the local area, which in turn will shift surface flow \nto southeasterly and bring gradually milder temperatures over \nthe course of the weekend and into early next week into the\nregion. As a result, highs in the 40s over the weekend will \nlikely yield to widespread highs in the 50s by Tuesday. As far \nas precipitation chances are concerned, persistent ridging and \nsurface high pressure across the region this weekend and into \nearly next week should keep any precipitation chances to a \nminimum. As a result, the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) generally has very low (under 25% chance) \nprobabilities for measurable rainfall this weekend through \nTuesday.",
